title: Persisted format changes
tags: Development
kind: reference
audience: dev
aliases: migration migrations
summary: Since version 0.3, a change to anything written on disk ships its conversion — existing repositories and configurations are migrated, never broken — and how each format does it today.

!! The rule

From version 0.3 on, a change to a ''persisted format'' comes with the path that
converts what already exists: a repository created by an earlier version must load,
and a configuration must keep working. Before 0.3, breaking the development
repositories was acceptable; it no longer is.

The rule protects ''data on disk'' only. The HTTP routes and the command tree may
still change without a deprecation period — that is what
[[The wire protocol version]] announces.

Prefer a conversion at opening, which nobody has to run; write a dedicated command
when it cannot be done there. Either way the conversion is tested on a repository of
the old format, and an up-to-date one must be opened without being rewritten
([[Key invariants]]).

!! What is persisted, and how each changes

|!Format |!Where |!How it migrates |
|the store's primary tables |`.metafolder/internal/kv/` ([[Store tables]]) |`primary_version` in the `version` table (`layout` in the `meta` table up to layout 10, still read to recognise an older store): a store of another version is converted when it is opened, in one transaction (`KvStore::migrate_layout`). Layout 3 (October 2026, the log stops being a tree) ''empties the log'' of an older store instead of converting it — the metarecords are kept, the history dropped: a breach of the rule decided by the user ([[The operation log]]). So do layout 4 (the operations split by origin for [[Automatic retention]]) layout 5 (labels in place of revisions, [[Log contexts]]) and layout 6 (the reverts of an operation indexed), the same way. Layout 7 (November 2026, a `forest` entry holds its metarecord's dense id, [[Deleting a metarecord in the store]]) is a true conversion: the ids are written into the entries and the log is kept. Layout 8 (November 2026, a record's rows under one key, its dense id, in `records`, [[Store tables]]) is another: the rows of `cells` and `field_cells` are grouped by metarecord and written under their id — a metarecord with none is given one, and the indexes are then derived again —, the forest entries lose their row id, and the log is kept (a store of layouts 6 and 7; the older ones lose it, as before). Layout 9 (November 2026, table `label_reverts`: what a label reverts and what reverts it, [[Log context summaries]]) ''empties the log'' of an older store as layouts 3 to 6 did, the metarecords kept — the user's decision, since a revert of an older log has no such mark to read. Layout 10 (November 2026, an operation keeps its version delta and no longer the rows it left, [[Log storage]]) ''empties the log'' of an older store the same way, the metarecords kept — the user's decision: an older operation has no delta. Entries of writes to many records (November 2026, table `mass`) and the numbers a record then carries change no layout: the table is created when a store opens, and a record's value gains an optional tail that an older value does not have, which reads as no numbers. Since November 2026 a metarecord's version and an operation's two versions are no longer written ([[Metarecord version]]). Layout 11 (November 2026, the review of the store's tables, [[Store tables]]) is a true conversion of the data and ''empties the log'' (user decision): `meta` is split into `version`, `global_primary_state` and `global_derived_state`; every record is written again in the compact encoding ([[Value encoding in the store]]) under `metarecords` — `records` renamed, the former `metarecords` gone, a metarecord with no row given an empty record —, a field holding `nothing` beside values loses the `nothing` ([[Nothing versus unknown]]); `ids` and the universe (now `live_metarecords`) become primary and are carried over, the reserved ids kept in `ids`; the pending writes of `mass` are folded before the conversion; the derived tables are derived again. `meta.layout` is still read to recognise an older store; it goes, with every conversion older than layout 11, once the user's stores are converted (user decision). The tables of the older layouts (`parts`, `cells`, `field_cells`, `row_owner`, `by_field`, `op_children`, `restorations`, `ops_by_rev`, `revisions`) are emptied by the conversion and ''removed'' when the store opens — a named table, even empty, stays in the file's catalog and counts against `max_dbs` — a store converted before that loses them at its next open |
|the store's derived key spaces |the same file |`derived_version` in `version` (`derived` in `meta` before layout 11): on a difference they are derived again from the primary tables — a reindex ''is'' their migration (derived 11 to 15, November 2026: every value a posting and no value partition, a position's name a posting and no `parts`, a position's value none, the posting keys of a bucket counted in the histogram, the record of a deleted metarecord kept until a reindex — the next open of an older store derives them again) |
|a row's value |inside the store, the pending writes and the log ([[Value encoding in the store]]) |changes only with a layout migration |
|the repository's configuration |`.metafolder/config.json` ([[Repository layout on disk]]) |a `version` field; a new key is optional with a default, so an older file reads unchanged |
|a repository's configuration files |`.metafolder/config/` ([[Repository configuration]]) |a git merge of the defaults into the repository's branch, by `mf repo update-config`. The October 2026 move of `schema.json` and `metadata-map.toml` from `.metafolder/` into `config/` is the same command's first run: the daemon refuses a repository of the old layout at load and names it — not a conversion at opening, since the daemon writes no configuration file |
|the user's configuration |`~/.config/metafolder/` ([[The configuration repository]]) |a git merge of the shipped defaults into the user's branch at each update ([[Updating the configuration]]). What the merge cannot reach is converted after it: a `{target}` template the user wrote in `gui/keybindings.toml` (October 2026, the template removed) is rewritten as its four lines, and a variable naming another holds its keys written out, whenever the sync finds one. A key alone hiding a sequence, refused at load since the same month, has ''no conversion'' — which of the two to keep is the user's choice —: the GUI refuses the file and names both lines |
|the trash's index |`.metafolder/internal/trash/index/` ([[Trash on-disk layout]]) |''not migrated'': the SQLite index it replaced (`index.sqlite`) is not read, so what was trashed before the change stays on disk as blobs the index does not know — a breach of the rule, noted here. A path or a name in an entry is a string or, since November 2026, `{text, bytes}` when it is not text: an older entry's strings read as before, nothing to convert |
|backups |[[Backups and restore]] |a backup is a store: it migrates when it is restored and opened |
|a pair's sync state |a store per pair under `internal/`, with a `format_version` in its `meta` table |nothing reads the version yet: a change of format would have to. A snapshot's `datetime` is written to the millisecond since November 2026; an older one holds a whole second and reads as before, its file's mtime matching any millisecond of that second ([[Change detection in sync]]) — without that, every link would look changed on both sides once, and every file pair be read |
|a saved workspace |`.metafolder/gui/workspaces/<name>.json` ([[Saved workspaces]]) |a `version` field; a file of a newer version is refused with what reads it, not misread. Version 2 (the panel type `file` renamed `viewer`) converts a version 1 file as it is read, without rewriting it; version 3 (the metarecord list's two query fields AND-ed) converts a version 2 `work` save the same way |
|the benchmark history |`benchmarks/history/*.jsonl` ([[The timed regression suite]]) |rewritten in place when a field goes |

!! The precedent: SQLite to the key-value store

The largest change so far replaced the storage backend. Its conversion was the
migration: a faithful copy — every row, operation and revision under its own id — in
one transaction, both stores compared by digest before the switch
([[How the storage backend was built]]). SQLite was removed only once no real
repository was left on it, and a repository still on it is refused at load with
what to do, not opened wrong.

When a format cannot be read any more, that is the minimum: a refusal that names the
version able to convert it.
